<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  FORKED FROM m6/gazebo/forklift_ver2/model.sdf FOR THE m5-ver3 TRACK,
  2026-08-25, and byte-identical to it but for the model name below and
  this note. m5-ver3 is the sensor-fusion rebuild of ONE showcase vehicle
  (vault AMR-DEC-003, tasks/TODO.md m5-ver3); the plan it belongs to is
  .superpowers/sdd/2026-08-25-m5v3-f1-sensors, Task 1.

  forklift_ver2 IS NOT MODIFIED AND NEVER WILL BE BY THIS TRACK. The m6
  fleet is measured on it and a model edited under a shipped figure is
  not a model. The two files will drift - phase F1 replaces this one's
  ideal sensors with instrument profiles (TiM571 nav lidar, nanoScan3
  envelopes, an RGB-D camera) and takes the ground-truth odometry away
  in favour of wheel odometry through an EKF - and that drift is the
  whole point of the fork.

  TASK 1 LEFT THIS FILE BYTE-IDENTICAL BUT FOR ITS NAME; TASK 2 IS WHERE
  THE DRIFT STARTS, and it is this much of it:

    . nav_lidar carries a SICK TiM571 profile - 15 Hz, 811 rays over
      270 deg, 0.05 to 25.0 m, gaussian noise with a per-run bias -
      instead of the ideal 10 Hz / 360 deg / 8.0 m scanner. The device
      quantizes its range telegram to a millimetre and THIS MODEL DOES
      NOT; the sensor block below says why, under WHAT IS NOT MODELLED.
    . the three safety_scanner_* sensors gain a gaussian noise block AND
      NOTHING ELSE. Their aperture, sample count, rate, range and topic
      are frozen by the field contract the PLC side is written against.
    . nav_lidar_3d, an Ouster OS0 class 3D lidar on the guard roof, and
      pallet_cam, a RealSense D455 class RGB-D camera on the mast, are
      NEW. Neither existed on forklift_ver2.
    . the drive wheel carries gz-sim-wheel-slip-system, so a wheel
      revolution stops being exactly 2 pi r of ground.

  The IMU is untouched: its noise was already derived from a data sheet
  in m5_ver2 and there was nothing for this phase to improve. The
  ground-truth OdometryPublisher is also untouched and stays a measurement
  reference; taking it out of the estimator is F2's job, not this one's,
  and the two-phase note at the foot of this file already says so.
-->
<!--
  model.sdf . forklift_ver2: the in house tricycle forklift of
  agv/forklift/model.sdf, carrying THREE safety scanners instead of two.

  This is a COPY. agv/forklift/model.sdf is not modified and keeps its own
  two-scanner layout; the M5 stack still spawns that one. The two files
  will drift, and that is intended: this one is m5_ver2 Step 5's.

  WHAT CHANGED FROM THE ORIGINAL, and nothing else did:
    . The original's two corner scanner links, at (0.70, 0.45) and
      (.0.70, .0.45), with their sensors and their two fixed joints, are
      DELETED. Their names survive nowhere in this file, on purpose: a
      grep for either of them must return nothing.
    . safety_scanner_back_link, _left_link and _right_link replace them,
      named in the OWNER'S FRAME (see FRAMES below), with three fixed
      joints of their own.
    . Their range max is 8.0 m, not the 5.5 m the deleted pair used.

  This file is GEOMETRY, ACTUATION AND SENSING. It contains no control
  logic: no sequencing, no interlock, no timer, no latch, no threshold and
  no zone decision. A joint moves when a command topic says so, and the
  scanner reports raw ranges whatever stands in front of it. Deciding what
  any of that means belongs to the vehicle nodes, to the fleet manager or
  to the PLC, never here (invariants 5, 6 and 9).

  It is also NOT a safety device. Three of the four scanners below are
  named safety_scanner_*, and that names the DEVICE CLASS THEY MODEL, not
  a property of this file: a rendered depth image is not a protective
  field, has no integrity, no OSSD, no fault reaction and no diagnostic
  coverage. The protective stop, the e stop chain and safe torque off are
  onboard, hardwired and independent of anything in this file or on any
  network (invariant 1). What the three safety scanners give this project
  is the GEOMETRY of a real installation plus their NON-SAFE MEASUREMENT
  channels, which a field evaluation outside this file turns into
  protective and warning field verdicts. Neither of those is a safety
  property of this file, and nothing else about a safety device is
  modelled here. No Category, Performance Level, SIL or PFH is claimed.

  Original geometry, primitives only, no external mesh.

  ============================ FRAMES ============================

  Model origin: on the floor, at the centre of the chassis footprint.
    +x  forward, the driving direction, the steer and drive end
    +y  left
    +z  up

  Three wheels. The single steered wheel is also the driven wheel and it
  leads; the two passive wheels trail. The load is therefore carried at
  the .x end and the counterweight sits over the drive wheel, opposite
  the load, which is what a counterweight is for.

  THE SCANNER NAMES BELOW ARE THE OWNER'S FRAME, NOT THIS ONE. The
  owner's reference drawing calls the FORK end Front, which is this
  model's .x, and their frame is therefore this one rotated 180 deg about
  z: the owner's Left is model .y and the owner's Right is model +y. The
  deleted pair was named the other way round . the original file called
  the DRIVE end "front" and put the link named for it at +0.70 . and that
  disagreement is why the pair was deleted rather than kept. Two
  conventions in one file eventually wires the wrong device to the PLC.

  TOP VIEW (metres). SB, SL and SR are the three safety scanners; N is
  the navigation lidar, on the overhead guard, offset to .y of the mast
  centreline; N3 is the 3D lidar on the roof centre and C the pallet
  camera, outboard on the mast and looking at .x. SL is drawn BELOW the
  +x axis and SR above it, per the frame note above.

                            +y
                             ^
      fork tines             |            counterweight
     .......                 |               [====]
     |     | C     SR   [rear wheel L]           |
     |     |    [mast]  ......N3.....            |
   . | . . | . .[||||]. |  chassis  | . [steer + drive wheel] . SB . > +x
     |     |            |1.4 x 0.9  |     N (0.55, .0.40) at z 1.80
     |     |       SL   ......... ...            |
     .......            [rear wheel R]           |

     SB  safety_scanner_back   ( 0.72,  0.00)  link yaw    0 deg
     SL  safety_scanner_left   (.0.68, .0.46)  link yaw .135 deg
     SR  safety_scanner_right  (.0.68,  0.46)  link yaw  135 deg
     N   nav_lidar             ( 0.55, .0.40)  link yaw    0 deg
     N3  nav_lidar_3d          ( 0.00,  0.00)  link yaw    0 deg
     C   pallet_cam            (.0.90,  0.40)  link yaw  180 deg,
                                               pitch 30 deg down

  SIDE VIEW (metres)

                       N3  z = 1.85
                          N  z = 1.80
          overhead guard roof z = 1.71
          ._______________________.
          |                       |
     [||] |                       |          mast rails to z = 2.05
   C [||] |       chassis         |[ CW ]    chassis underside z = 0.20
     [||] |_______________________|          C  z = 1.10, looking .x
    [carriage]                    |             and 30 deg down
   ==========      SB/SL/SR safety scan plane z = 0.15
   forks     (o)             (o)  (o)   wheels r = 0.12
   ================================================ floor z = 0

  ========================= COMMAND TOPICS =========================

  Every gz system below names its topic EXPLICITLY. The model scoped
  defaults (/model/<name>/joint/<joint>/cmd_*) are deliberately not used:
  they change if the model is spawned under another name, and the ROS
  bridge, the launch file and agv/forklift/README.md all quote these
  names as a contract.

    /forklift/gz/actuator/steer_cmd     gz.msgs.Double  steer angle    [rad]
    /forklift/gz/actuator/traction_cmd  gz.msgs.Double  wheel rate   [rad/s]
    /forklift/gz/actuator/fork_cmd      gz.msgs.Double  carriage      [m]

      THESE ARE THE MOTOR TERMINALS, AND THEY ARE NOT WHAT THE VEHICLE
      STACK PUBLISHES (m5-50). The stack still publishes
      /forklift/gz/steer_cmd, /forklift/gz/traction_cmd and
      /forklift/gz/fork_cmd; agv/forklift/scripts/sto_contactor.py is the
      only publisher of the three terminals above and it is what joins
      them, one message for one message, while torque is present.

      WHY THE MODEL MOVED AND NOT THE STACK. On the safety program's
      TorqueOffDemand the second stage of SS1 has to make this vehicle
      DEAF TO COMMANDS and keep it deaf if the envelope reopens, or SS1's
      two stages collapse into the controlled stop the vehicle already
      has and no test can tell them apart. Five committed publishers
      address the command topics directly, so an interlock inside any one
      command node would be bypassed by the other four. An interlock at
      the MODEL's own inputs cannot be bypassed by anything that speaks
      ROS.

      THE CONTACTOR IS A STAND-IN, and every artefact says so. The safety
      path it models is hardwired and onboard (CLAUDE.md invariant 1);
      what is built here is a simulation of that path's effect on the
      plant. No Category, Performance Level, SIL or PFH is claimed for it
      or implied by it (ADR 0011 D5).

      WHAT TORQUE REMOVAL IS AT THIS PLANT, in the two halves the design
      spec names. The CONTROLLER IS DISABLED in the sense that no command
      from any publisher reaches any terminal while the latch is open. The
      HOLDING BRAKE is the traction terminal driven to a standing zero
      rather than left silent: the JointController below holds its last
      command for ever, so a silent terminal is a standing order and not
      an absence, and a standing zero is what pins the shaft. The steer
      and fork terminals are held at their last forwarded value, because
      zeroing them would command a straightening manoeuvre and a carriage
      descent - motion demanded BY the safe state, which is the one thing
      it must not do.

    /forklift/gz/joint_state   gz.msgs.Model    joint positions and rates

    /forklift/gz/drive_speed/read_a  gz.msgs.Model  drive shaft, reading A
    /forklift/gz/drive_speed/read_b  gz.msgs.Model  drive shaft, reading B
      Two reading channels on ONE shaft, cross-compared downstream: a
      SINGLE-CHANNEL TESTED SYSTEM, never a two-channel one. See the
      block beside the two systems at the foot of this file.

    /forklift/gz/odom          gz.msgs.Odometry GROUND-TRUTH pose and twist
    /forklift/gz/tf_ground_truth  gz.msgs.Pose_V  odom -> base_link, from
                                                  the same ground truth

    /forklift/gz/scan_nav           gz.msgs.LaserScan  270 deg, 15 Hz,
                                                       z = 1.80, TiM571
    /forklift/gz/points3d           gz.msgs.LaserScan  1024 x 32, 10 Hz,
                                                       z = 1.85, OS0 class
    /forklift/gz/points3d/points    gz.msgs.PointCloudPacked, the same
                                                       sweep as a cloud -
                                                       gz advertises it
                                                       because the scan
                                                       has 32 rows
    /forklift/gz/cam/image          gz.msgs.Image      640 x 480 colour
    /forklift/gz/cam/depth_image    gz.msgs.Image      640 x 480 depth
    /forklift/gz/cam/camera_info    gz.msgs.CameraInfo
    /forklift/gz/cam/points         gz.msgs.PointCloudPacked
                                                       D455 class, on the
                                                       mast, 15 Hz. The
                                                       DEPTH IMAGE and the
                                                       CAMERA INFO are
                                                       what F1 bridges;
                                                       neither cloud is.
    /forklift/gz/imu                gz.msgs.IMU        6-axis MEMS, 100 Hz,
                                                       NO orientation output

    /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_back/measurement    275 deg, z = 0.15
    /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_left/measurement    275 deg, z = 0.15
    /forklift/gz/safety_scanner_right/measurement   275 deg, z = 0.15

  TWO CHANNELS PER SAFETY SCANNER, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A TOPIC. The
  device class these three sensors model emits a safe output (OSSD pair,
  or safe bits over PROFIsafe) AND a separate NON-SAFE measurement channel
  that the datasheet provides for HMI, diagnostics and process use while
  stating it must not be used for safety-related tasks. The gpu_lidar
  scan below IS the measurement channel; the safe channel is DERIVED from
  it by field evaluation, which is what the real device does internally,
  and that derivation is not in this file. Hence the rule a later reader
  can check mechanically:

    every channel a subscriber can reach is a MEASUREMENT channel.
    The safe channel has no topic, on gz transport or on ROS, ever.

  The measurement channel is non-safe and must never implement a safety
  function. The safe channel reaches the F-program, and it never reaches
  it through a topic, on either transport, under any path.

  BY WHICH PATH IT REACHES THE F-PROGRAM IS DESIGN INTENT, NOT SETTLED
  FACT. ADR 0011 decision 2 makes it configured F-I/O, an ET 200SP F-DI
  parameterised as the scanner's OSSD pair, whose channel values the
  S7-PLCSIM Advanced API drives by tag name: this project's analogue of
  the copper an OSSD pair runs on. That path has never been run. The two
  tool questions it rests on, whether this PLCSIM Advanced version and
  its safety system version simulate F-I/O at all, and whether the API
  writes a configured F-DI's channel values by tag name, are settled in
  the tool by plc/forklift-safety/FIO-FEASIBILITY.md under brief m5-03,
  whose verdict section is blank as this is written. If either answers
  no, the named fallback of ADR 0011 decision 2 is the standard-DB
  stand-in of plc/forklift-safety/SPEC.md, labelled a stand-in wherever
  it appears. Nothing in THIS file changes under either answer: the safe
  channel is derived outside this file by a field evaluation and is not
  a topic either way.

  The numeric values a node needs (wheel radius, steer limit, fork travel)
  are mirrored in config.yaml because SDF cannot be read as YAML. THIS
  FILE IS THE AUTHORITY for the geometry; config.yaml records the same
  numbers for the Python side and is diffed against the values below.

  Sensor note: all FIVE scanners are gz "gpu_lidar" sensors, because
  gz sim has no CPU ray sensor, and the pallet camera is an
  "rgbd_camera". All six RENDER. The world that spawns this model MUST
  load gz-sim-sensors-system with a render engine. gz's stock empty.sdf
  does not; sim/worlds/cell.sdf does, and so does
  m6/gazebo/warehouse_ver3.sdf, which is the floor this track spawns
  onto. See README.md.

  ONE SENSOR, ONE LINK, AND THE ROTATION LIVES IN THE LINK POSE. Each
  scanner has its own link and its <gz_frame_id> names that link. Without
  gz_frame_id the message carries a scoped model/link/sensor frame id and
  a downstream TF lookup fails. The aperture rotation of the three safety
  scanners is written into the LINK pose and the sensor pose is identity,
  so the frame the message names is also the frame its angles are
  measured in. Rotating the sensor inside its link would leave the two
  disagreeing by the mount angle, with nothing to say so.
-->
<sdf version="1.8">
  <model name="forklift_ver3">
    <static>false</static>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>

    <!-- ========================= Chassis ========================= -->
    <!--
      One rigid body carrying the body shell, the counterweight, the
      overhead guard and the safety scanner. The guard is visual only:
      nothing in this project drives under an obstruction, and four thin
      collision posts would cost solver time for no observable behaviour.
    -->
    <link name="base_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0.10 0 0.55 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>900.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>277.5</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>363.75</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>207.75</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="chassis_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>1.40 0.90 0.50</size></box></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.6</mu><mu2>0.6</mu2></ode></friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="chassis_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>1.40 0.90 0.50</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.90 0.42 0.05 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.42 0.05 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Counterweight, over the drive wheel and opposite the load. -->
      <collision name="counterweight_collision">
        <pose>0.74 0 0.51 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.24 0.80 0.42</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="counterweight_visual">
        <pose>0.74 0 0.51 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.24 0.80 0.42</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.28 0.04 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.62 0.28 0.04 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Overhead guard: four posts and a roof plate, visual only. -->
      <visual name="guard_post_fl">
        <pose>0.55 0.38 1.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.07 0.07 0.95</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="guard_post_fr">
        <pose>0.55 -0.38 1.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.07 0.07 0.95</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="guard_post_rl">
        <pose>-0.55 0.38 1.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.07 0.07 0.95</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="guard_post_rr">
        <pose>-0.55 -0.38 1.175 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.07 0.07 0.95</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="guard_roof">
        <pose>0 0 1.68 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>1.24 0.88 0.06</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>

    </link>

    <!-- ======================== Sensing ========================= -->
    <!--
      Four planar scanners, each on its own link: three safety scanners
      and the navigation lidar - and, since F1 Task 2, a 3D lidar and an
      RGB-D camera on two links of their own after them. THE THREE SAFETY
      SCANNERS ARE NAMED IN THE OWNER'S FRAME, WHICH IS THIS MODEL'S
      ROTATED 180 deg ABOUT z. The owner's Front is the FORK end, the
      model's -x; the owner's Left is the model's -y. Their placement
      comes from the owner's reference drawing and not from
      EVIDENCE_SENSOR_COVERAGE.md, which measured the superseded
      two-scanner layout of agv/forklift/model.sdf. The navigation
      lidar's MOUNT is unchanged and its derivation is still that
      document's; the sensor on that mount is not, and its own block says
      what replaced it.

      SCAN PLANE z = 0.15 for the three safety scanners. The free window
      at the fork end is bounded below by the lowered tine top (z = 0.10,
      fork_left/right at z 0.075 with 0.05 height) and above by the
      chassis underside (z = 0.20, chassis box centred 0.45 with 0.50
      height). 0.15 is the midpoint: 50 mm of clearance each way.

      POSITIONS. Back sits on the rear face at (0.72, 0), just outside the
      chassis footprint's +0.70 edge. Left and Right sit at the fork-end
      corners, (.0.68, .0.46) and (.0.68, 0.46), on the footprint corners
      of the 1.40 x 0.90 chassis.

      APERTURE 275 deg, modelled on a SICK microScan3 Pro, with the 85 deg
      blind sector pointed into the vehicle body in every case: link yaw 0
      at Back, so its blind sector is centred on 180 deg; .135 deg at Left
      and +135 deg at Right, so each corner scanner's blind sector points
      back across the vehicle.

      NAVIGATION LIDAR z = 1.80, on the overhead guard. This one is
      forced, not chosen. A 360 deg aperture is only worth its render
      cost where the sensor clears the vehicle body, and the two best low
      mountings measured lose 168.5 and 171.9 deg of it to that body,
      against 29.0 deg here. Above the guard roof (top z = 1.71) and
      below the mast crossmember (z = 1.95) the only permanent occluder
      left is the mast. The .y lateral offset from the mast centreline
      narrows that shadow and clears the fork axis. The height has a cost
      of its own, written up in the evidence: at 1.80 m this sensor is
      above every feature of sim/worlds/forklift_arena.sdf as it stands
      except one pillar.

      RAW RANGES ONLY, all four. No zone, no threshold and no stop
      decision is taken in this file. m5_ver2/step5/ipc/field_eval.py
      evaluates the protective and warning fields over these measurement
      channels, outside this file, exactly as the real device does that
      derivation internally.

      CONSUMERS, PER CHANNEL, AS THEY STAND. All three safety measurement
      channels are bridged into ROS and read by the field evaluator; the
      BACK verdict is the one that reaches the F-PLC's PF_OSSD and
      WF_Clear. No device's safe channel is a topic on any transport,
      because the modelled counterpart's safe output is an OSSD pair on
      copper; it is derived from the same rays as the measurement channel
      by a field evaluation that is not in this file, and that shared
      origin is not a second independent channel (agv/forklift/README.md,
      "Two channels per safety scanner"). The NAVIGATION lidar is the
      SLAM, AMCL and costmap input, and no scanner channel of either kind
      reaches a navigation consumer.

      BUDGET, and the rule that governs it. Rendering on the target
      machine is llvmpipe software rasterisation
      (sim/setup/WSL_ENVIRONMENT.md 4.7), so the vehicle's ray budget is
      held down: 275 + 275 + 275 + 360 = 1185 rays per 100 ms across four
      sensors, up from 910 across three when there were two safety
      scanners. That cost has NOT been measured. No sample count and no
      update rate here goes up until someone measures the real time factor
      it buys and writes the figure down.
    -->

    <link name="safety_scanner_back_link">
      <!-- BACK in the owner's frame: the drive/counterweight end, model +x,
           centred on the rear face. Yaw 0 points the sensor x axis straight
           out, so the 85 deg blind sector is centred on 180 deg, into the
           vehicle. The owner's Front is the FORK end, model -x. -->
      <pose>0.72 0 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.004</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.004</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Housing top face sits 20 mm BELOW the scan plane, so the scanner
           cannot see its own mounting. Visual only. -->
      <visual name="housing">
        <pose>0 0 -0.075 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.14 0.14 0.11</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="safety_scanner_back" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>safety_scanner_back_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <!-- NON-SAFE MEASUREMENT CHANNEL. The safe channel is not here and
             is not a topic: 1oo2 is the fail-safe input card's property, not
             the simulation's, and in PLCSIM the pair collapses to one
             process-image bit (m5_ver2/CLAUDE.md section 3).

             RANGE MAX IS 8.0, NOT THE 5.5 THE OLD SCANNERS USED. The debug
             script's case 3 warning field is 6.0 m. Capped at 5.5 the test
             `d > 6.0` can never pass, so the warning field would read as
             permanently violated in the very case the system falls into
             when the monitoring case is unreadable. -->
        <topic>/forklift/gz/safety_scanner_back/measurement</topic>
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>275</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-2.3998277</min_angle>
              <max_angle>2.3998277</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>0.0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.0</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.10</min>
            <max>8.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <!-- THE ONLY THING F1 CHANGED ABOUT THIS SENSOR. nanoScan3-class
               statistical error, 20 mm, as white noise on the range.
               Everything else here - the aperture, the 275 samples, the
               10 Hz, the 0.10 to 8.0 m and the topic - is frozen: the
               field evaluation outside this file and the PLC's own case
               table are written against those numbers, and a scanner
               whose geometry moves under a committed field is a different
               device. Plain gaussian and no bias term: what the safety
               layer needs to see is that a range reading is not exact,
               and a per-device systematic offset is not something any
               artefact downstream of here has measured. -->
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.02</stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <link name="safety_scanner_left_link">
      <!-- LEFT in the owner's frame is the model's -y, because the owner's
           frame is the model's rotated 180 deg about z. Mounted at the
           fork-end corner; yaw -135 deg points the sensor out along the
           corner diagonal. -->
      <pose>-0.68 -0.46 0.15 0 0 -2.3561945</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.004</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.004</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Housing top face sits 20 mm BELOW the scan plane, so the scanner
           cannot see its own mounting. Visual only. -->
      <visual name="housing">
        <pose>0 0 -0.075 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.14 0.14 0.11</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="safety_scanner_left" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>safety_scanner_left_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <!-- NON-SAFE MEASUREMENT CHANNEL. The safe channel is not here and
             is not a topic: 1oo2 is the fail-safe input card's property, not
             the simulation's, and in PLCSIM the pair collapses to one
             process-image bit (m5_ver2/CLAUDE.md section 3).

             RANGE MAX IS 8.0, NOT THE 5.5 THE OLD SCANNERS USED. The debug
             script's case 3 warning field is 6.0 m. Capped at 5.5 the test
             `d > 6.0` can never pass, so the warning field would read as
             permanently violated in the very case the system falls into
             when the monitoring case is unreadable. -->
        <topic>/forklift/gz/safety_scanner_left/measurement</topic>
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>275</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-2.3998277</min_angle>
              <max_angle>2.3998277</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>0.0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.0</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.10</min>
            <max>8.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <!-- THE ONLY THING F1 CHANGED ABOUT THIS SENSOR. nanoScan3-class
               statistical error, 20 mm, as white noise on the range.
               Everything else here - the aperture, the 275 samples, the
               10 Hz, the 0.10 to 8.0 m and the topic - is frozen: the
               field evaluation outside this file and the PLC's own case
               table are written against those numbers, and a scanner
               whose geometry moves under a committed field is a different
               device. Plain gaussian and no bias term: what the safety
               layer needs to see is that a range reading is not exact,
               and a per-device systematic offset is not something any
               artefact downstream of here has measured. -->
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.02</stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <link name="safety_scanner_right_link">
      <!-- RIGHT in the owner's frame is the model's +y, because the owner's
           frame is the model's rotated 180 deg about z. Mounted at the
           fork-end corner; yaw +135 deg points the sensor out along the
           corner diagonal. -->
      <pose>-0.68 0.46 0.15 0 0 2.3561945</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.4</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.004</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.004</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Housing top face sits 20 mm BELOW the scan plane, so the scanner
           cannot see its own mounting. Visual only. -->
      <visual name="housing">
        <pose>0 0 -0.075 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.14 0.14 0.11</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.55 0.05 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="safety_scanner_right" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>safety_scanner_right_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <!-- NON-SAFE MEASUREMENT CHANNEL. The safe channel is not here and
             is not a topic: 1oo2 is the fail-safe input card's property, not
             the simulation's, and in PLCSIM the pair collapses to one
             process-image bit (m5_ver2/CLAUDE.md section 3).

             RANGE MAX IS 8.0, NOT THE 5.5 THE OLD SCANNERS USED. The debug
             script's case 3 warning field is 6.0 m. Capped at 5.5 the test
             `d > 6.0` can never pass, so the warning field would read as
             permanently violated in the very case the system falls into
             when the monitoring case is unreadable. -->
        <topic>/forklift/gz/safety_scanner_right/measurement</topic>
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>275</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-2.3998277</min_angle>
              <max_angle>2.3998277</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>0.0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.0</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.10</min>
            <max>8.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <!-- THE ONLY THING F1 CHANGED ABOUT THIS SENSOR. nanoScan3-class
               statistical error, 20 mm, as white noise on the range.
               Everything else here - the aperture, the 275 samples, the
               10 Hz, the 0.10 to 8.0 m and the topic - is frozen: the
               field evaluation outside this file and the PLC's own case
               table are written against those numbers, and a scanner
               whose geometry moves under a committed field is a different
               device. Plain gaussian and no bias term: what the safety
               layer needs to see is that a range reading is not exact,
               and a per-device systematic offset is not something any
               artefact downstream of here has measured. -->
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.02</stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <link name="nav_lidar_link">
      <!-- On the overhead guard roof, offset to .y of the mast
           centreline. THE MOUNT IS THE ORIGINAL'S TO THE MILLIMETRE and
           the topic is too; what F1 Task 2 replaced is the SENSOR on it,
           from the ideal 360 deg / 10 Hz / 8.0 m scanner m5_ver2 shipped
           to a SICK TiM571 profile - the block inside <sensor> below is
           the derivation. The 8.0 m range max that
           EVIDENCE_SENSOR_COVERAGE.md 1 left open for a 24 x 16 m arena
           goes with it, answered by the device's own 25.0 m rather than
           by a choice. The three SAFETY scanners keep the 8.0 m they
           took from this sensor: their geometry is frozen by the PLC
           field contract and F1 may not touch it.

           The mount stands over the front right overhead guard post
           (0.55, .0.38), the stiffest point of the roof plate, displaced
           20 mm further outboard: at y = .0.40 the straight astern ray
           passes 0.04 m clear of the mast body, and .0.40 is also the
           largest offset that keeps the 0.06 post inside the 1.24 x 0.88
           roof plate. Measured in EVIDENCE_SENSOR_COVERAGE.md 7. -->
      <pose>0.55 -0.40 1.80 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.002</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.002</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.002</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Post and head both finish BELOW the scan plane, as the three
           safety scanner housings do, so no sensor renders its own
           mounting. -->
      <visual name="post">
        <pose>0 0 -0.065 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.11</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="head">
        <pose>0 0 -0.040 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.05</radius><length>0.04</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.10 0.10 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.10 0.10 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="nav_lidar" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>nav_lidar_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <visualize>true</visualize>
        <topic>/forklift/gz/scan_nav</topic>
        <!--
          SICK TiM571-2050101, data sheet 1075091, through
          docs/reports/m5v3-03 section 1 and its pinned source 5, read
          2026-08-25. Every number below is that device's, and where a
          data sheet figure is not used raw the arithmetic is written out.

            update_rate 15   the device's own scan frequency. It replaces
                             10 Hz, and the cost of the extra five renders
                             a second was measured rather than assumed
                             (EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 6).

            samples 811      0.33 deg angular resolution over 270 deg:
                             270 / 0.33333 = 810 intervals, 811 rays.

            range 0.05-25.0  the device's working range, replacing an
                             8.0 m that was never this sensor's own
                             figure.

            resolution 0.01  unchanged.

          THE RAY COUNT GOING UP IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE LEVER THIS SENSOR
          USED TO CARRY, AND THE SAME MEASUREMENT COVERS BOTH. M6.6's spec
          named the ray count the one thing to change if the scans stopped
          keeping up, and on the new floor they had: 5.13 Hz per safety
          scanner with four trucks against a nominal 10. Measured
          2026-08-23 at 180 rays on the same stack: 5.07 Hz. Halving the
          rays bought nothing, because what that rig is bound by is the
          SCENE render gz runs once per sensor and not the ray count
          within it - which is also why 360 -> 811 here is expected to be
          nearly free and 10 -> 15 Hz is not. Expected, then measured.

          THE NOISE IS THE DATA SHEET'S TWO ERROR ROWS, and they are two
          DIFFERENT errors, which is why they land on two different
          elements. THESE TWO ARE THE WHOLE OF WHAT THIS SENSOR
          CONFIGURES: the type is plain gaussian and there is no third
          element.

            stddev 0.02       statistical error, < 20 mm at 90 percent
                              remission. Shot-to-shot spread, so it is the
                              per-ray white noise.

            bias_stddev 0.02  systematic error, +-60 mm. A systematic
                              error is a property of the DEVICE and does
                              not resample per ray, so it is drawn once as
                              a bias; +-60 mm read as a 3 sigma bracket
                              puts that draw's sigma at 20 mm.

          THE BIAS PAIR IS NOT DECLARED BY THE SCHEMA, and is honoured
          anyway. sdformat's lidar.sdf writes its own inline <noise>
          element carrying only type, mean and stddev - in every SDF
          version, 1.11 included - so gz warns at load that bias_mean and
          bias_stddev are "not defined in SDF", copies them through, and
          gz-sensors reads them regardless. That they genuinely produce a
          bias, and a fresh draw of it every run, was MEASURED rather than
          assumed: EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 5.3.

          THE WINDOW, AND WHY IT IS NOT SYMMETRIC ABOUT THIS FRAME'S x.
          A TiM571 sees 270 deg and is blind over the other 90, and where
          that blind sector points is a MOUNTING decision, not a data
          sheet figure. This vehicle's travel direction is model -x: model
          yaw 0 points the forks at world -x, so the travel heading is
          yaw + pi and forward traction is negative (m6/ipc/follower.py,
          header). This link's pose carries no rotation, so bearing 0 in
          the message is model +x, the counterweight end, ASTERN of
          travel. The window is therefore centred on bearing pi:

            centre     pi          = 3.1415927   model -x, dead ahead
            half       270/2 deg   = 2.3561945
            min_angle              = 0.7853982
            max_angle              = 5.4977871

          The blind 90 deg is then centred on model +x and the vehicle
          drives into the full aperture. The symmetric alternative,
          -2.3561945 to 2.3561945, was rejected for exactly that reason:
          it points the blind sector at the direction of travel, the one
          bearing a navigation lidar may not lose. min_angle > 0 with
          max_angle > pi is legal SDF but it is not what anybody writes,
          so the offset window was checked against known world geometry
          rather than trusted - EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 4 carries both the
          check and the rejected alternative.

          WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, STATED RATHER THAN OMITTED. Three
          things, and the first of them is the one a reader of the noise
          block above would otherwise assume was here.

          THE MILLIMETRE QUANTIZATION. The measurement telegram reports
          in millimetres, so nothing the real device produces is finer
          than 1 mm - and nothing in this file reproduces that. The SDF
          element for it is <precision>, which ONLY the
          gaussian_quantized noise type carries, and gaussian_quantized
          was MEASURED on this rig to produce NO NOISE AT ALL on a
          gpu_lidar: zero temporal spread on all 710 usable beams, five
          consecutive sweeps byte-identical, and not one warning from gz.
          The choice was therefore between a quantized scan with no noise
          in it and a noisy scan with no quantization, and this file takes
          the second - <type>gaussian</type>, no <precision>, and the 1 mm
          grid named HERE, as absent. The modelled range is thus FINER
          than the device by 1 mm inside a channel whose noise is 20 mm,
          which is why it is a footnote and not a defect; it is not a
          silence either. EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 5.2 carries the
          measurement and 9.2 the root cause.

          REMISSION DEPENDENCE. The data sheet's statistical error is
          < 20 mm at 90 percent remission and < 10 mm at 10 percent to
          6 m, and gz's noise model has no reflectance term at all, so the
          LARGER figure is used and the modelled scan is the pessimistic
          one.

          GZ-SIM ISSUE #2743 (open, 2025-01-29). A gpu_lidar is measurably
          less accurate than a CPU ray sensor at shallow incidence, which
          is the rack leg and the pallet face seen obliquely. gz sim has
          no CPU ray sensor and there is no fix upstream, so it is written
          down here instead of worked around.
        -->
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>811</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>0.7853982</min_angle>
              <max_angle>5.4977871</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>1</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>0.0</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.0</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.05</min>
            <max>25.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.02</stddev>
            <bias_mean>0.0</bias_mean>
            <bias_stddev>0.02</bias_stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ====================== 3D navigation lidar ================ -->
    <!--
      OUSTER OS0 CLASS, AND IT IS THE ONE SENSOR HERE WITH NO ROS
      CONSUMER YET. F2 fuses it; F1 adds it, measures what it costs and
      bridges nothing (config.yaml topics.points3d says so in the file a
      script would read). A channel bridged before something consumes it
      is a claim the run does not make, and on this rig it is an
      expensive one: bridging lidar took ros_gz issue #368's rig from
      90 percent RTF to 40-60.

      WHERE IT SITS. On the overhead guard roof, centred: the roof plate
      is 1.24 x 0.88 at z 1.65 to 1.71 (base_link, guard_roof), so the
      mount face is z = 1.71 and the model centreline is x = 0, y = 0.
      The optical origin is 0.14 m above that face at z = 1.85, and the
      housing below it is drawn to finish at 1.84 - the same rule the
      four planar scanners follow, that no sensor renders its own
      mounting. It holds, and it was checked rather than assumed: see the
      row table below, where no reading in a whole sweep falls below the
      0.300 m range minimum.

      WHAT IT DOES SEE OF ITS OWN VEHICLE, because a 90 deg vertical
      aperture on a roof cannot not. This is MEASURED, one sweep, truck
      at rest, and the row-by-row table is EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 8. The 32
      rows are 2.903 deg apart from -45.00 to +45.00 and they read:

        rows  6 to  9   -27.58 to -18.87 deg   THE ROOF PLATE, a full
                        1024-bearing ring at 0.300 to 0.513 m. It is
                        0.14 m below the origin, so slant = 0.14/sin
                        (elevation) and the ring's inner edge is at the
                        range minimum exactly.
        rows 10 and 11  -15.97, -13.06 deg     the roof again but only
                        720 and 528 bearings of 1024: the plate is
                        1.24 x 0.88, so a shallower beam still reaches it
                        fore and aft and misses it abeam.
        row   5         -30.48 deg             the roof is at 0.276 m
                        here, inside the minimum, and 978 of 1024
                        bearings come back with NO READING at all.
        rows  0 to  4   -45.00 to -33.39 deg   44 to 153 returns each at
                        0.715 to 1.251 m: the four overhead-guard posts
                        and the mast.
        rows 12 to 20   -10.16 to +13.06 deg   44 to 144 returns each at
                        0.590 to 0.915 m: the mast rails at (-0.78,
                        +-0.30) rising to z 2.05, two narrow bearings
                        wide, and the crossmember above them.
        rows 27 to 31   +33.39 deg and up      nothing at all. The
                        warehouse has no ceiling.

      NOTHING IS ATTRIBUTABLE TO THIS SENSOR'S OWN POST OR HOUSING: both
      finish below z 1.84, within 0.09 m of the origin, and no reading
      anywhere in the sweep falls below the 0.300 m minimum.

      TWO gz BEHAVIOURS THE TABLE ABOVE PINS DOWN, both worth knowing
      before a consumer is written against this topic. A return closer
      than <range><min> comes back CLAMPED TO THE MINIMUM and not as a
      no-reading - 562 of the roof ring's readings are exactly 0.300 -
      and a return closer still comes back as a no-reading rather than
      passing through to whatever is behind it. So near geometry is
      neither invisible nor honest here; it is a wall of readings at the
      range minimum.

      NONE OF IT IS MASKED HERE and none of it may be: this file is
      geometry and sensing, and deciding which returns are the vehicle is
      the consumer's job (invariants 5, 6 and 9). It is written down so
      the F2 consumer does not discover it as a bug.

      THE NUMBERS ARE THE DEVICE CLASS's (docs/reports/m5v3-03 section 4,
      Balyo's reflectorless geoguidance runs an Ouster OS on exactly this
      duty):

        update_rate 10                    OS0 runs 10 or 20 Hz; 10 is the
                                          cheaper of the two supported
                                          rates and this rig pays per
                                          render.
        horizontal 1024 over 360 deg      the OS0's 1024-column mode.
        vertical 32 over 90 deg           OS0-32: 32 channels, 90 deg
                                          vertical aperture, the widest
                                          of the Ouster line and the
                                          reason this class is chosen for
                                          a vehicle that must see a rack
                                          upright and the floor at once.
        range 0.3-50.0                    minimum range and the OS0's
                                          practical maximum.
        noise gaussian stddev 0.025       range precision class, 15 to
                                          30 mm depending on target and
                                          distance; 25 mm is the middle
                                          of that band. NOT
                                          gaussian_quantized: the device
                                          reports range in millimetres
                                          too, but the quantization is
                                          two orders below the precision
                                          and modelling it would claim a
                                          fidelity nothing here has
                                          measured.

      32768 RAYS PER SWEEP IS THE LARGEST SINGLE COST IN THIS FILE and
      the RTF with and without it is measured, not argued
      (EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 6).
    -->
    <link name="nav_lidar_3d_link">
      <pose>0 0 1.85 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.5</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0004</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0004</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.0004</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- Post from the roof face at z 1.71 to the housing at 1.76, and
           the housing from 1.76 to 1.84 - everything below the optical
           origin at 1.85 and everything inside the 0.3 m near clip. -->
      <visual name="post">
        <pose>0 0 -0.115 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.06 0.06 0.05</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="housing">
        <pose>0 0 -0.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.045</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.10 0.10 0.12 1</ambient><diffuse>0.10 0.10 0.12 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="nav_lidar_3d" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>nav_lidar_3d_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <!-- FALSE, ALONE AMONG THE FIVE SCANNERS HERE, and it is a GUI
             hint and not a plant property. VisualizeLidar draws one
             marker per ray; 32768 of them at 10 Hz is a window that
             cannot keep up, and this track's figures are taken headless
             anyway. The other four stay true. -->
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <topic>/forklift/gz/points3d</topic>
        <lidar>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>1024</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-3.1415927</min_angle>
              <max_angle>3.1415927</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>32</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-0.7853982</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.7853982</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.3</min>
            <max>50.0</max>
            <resolution>0.01</resolution>
          </range>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.025</stddev>
          </noise>
        </lidar>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ========================= Pallet camera =================== -->
    <!--
      INTEL REALSENSE D455 CLASS, ON THE MAST, LOOKING DOWN THE FORKS.
      The vendors converged on this and the sim follows them: Fox
      Robotics builds pallet cameras into the MAST, Third Wave puts 3D
      cameras near the forks, Balyo carries a dedicated pallet camera
      beside its 3D lidar (docs/reports/m5v3-03 section 4).

      WHICH WAY IS "THE FORK DIRECTION". Model -x. Model yaw 0 points the
      forks at world -x and the travel heading is yaw + pi
      (m6/ipc/follower.py, header), and the tines in this file sit at
      x -1.875 to -0.825. A gz camera looks down its own +x, so the link
      carries yaw pi. It also carries pitch +0.5235988 (30 deg down), and
      that is not decoration:

        vertical half aperture = atan(tan(1.518/2) x 480/640) = 35.25 deg
        looking level from z 1.10 the floor would not enter the frame
          until 1.10 / tan(35.25) = 1.56 m, and the fork TIPS are only
          0.98 m ahead of the lens - a pallet camera that cannot see its
          own tines is not one
        pitched 30 deg down the visible floor band starts at
          1.10 / tan(65.25) = 0.51 m ahead (slant 1.21 m, past the 0.6 m
          near clip) and the optical axis meets the floor at 1.91 m
        the left tine tip is then 45.5 deg below the horizon and 7.0 deg
          off axis at 1.40 m, the right tine 34.9 deg off axis - both
          inside the 43.5 deg horizontal and 35.25 deg vertical halves

      WHERE IT SITS, AND WHY NOT ON THE CENTRELINE. The carriage is
      0.75 m wide and travels the whole mast, x -0.85 to -0.75, z 0.10 to
      2.20 - there is no height on this mast's centreline the carriage
      does not sweep. So the camera goes FORWARD of the carriage's
      travel, at x -0.90, and outboard at y 0.40, which keeps its 0.09 m
      housing inside the 0.90 m body width (0.355 to 0.445 against a
      half-width of 0.45). The bracket that holds it is two boxes in the
      MAST link, where they are axis-aligned and can be read against the
      carriage sweep: an arm at y 0.42, clear of the carriage's 0.375
      half-width, and a lateral plate ending at x -0.749, one millimetre
      clear of the carriage's -0.75 face.

      WHAT THE NEAR CLIP DOES TO THE VEHICLE, STATED AS THE ARTEFACT IT
      IS. Everything of this truck that lies ahead of the lens - the mast
      rails 0.13 m away, the carriage, the bracket - is inside the 0.6 m
      near clip, and a rasteriser does not occlude with geometry it has
      clipped: it renders through it. A real D455 mounted here would have
      the left rail across part of its view. This one does not, so the
      modelled camera sees MORE than the device would, and any framing of
      that as realism would be a lie. It is the same near clip the device
      has as its minimum depth, and the honest statement is that this
      camera has no opinion about anything closer than 0.6 m.

      THE NUMBERS (docs/reports/m5v3-03 section 1 and pinned source 9):

        640 x 480                 the D455 depth stream this class is
                                  quoted at.
        horizontal_fov 1.518      87 deg, the D455 depth field of view.
        clip 0.6-6.0              the device's minimum depth and its
                                  usable range. The far clip applies to
                                  the colour render too, which nothing
                                  consumes; beyond 6.0 m depth comes back
                                  as no reading, which is what the device
                                  does.
        update_rate 15            m5v3-03 section 5's budget for a pallet
                                  camera is 6 to 10 Hz at 320 x 240 for
                                  FOUR trucks. This track has ONE, and 15
                                  Hz at 640 x 480 is measured here rather
                                  than assumed affordable.

        noise gaussian stddev 0.008
                                  A FLOOR, AND SAID TO BE ONE. The real
                                  device's depth error is roughly
                                  quadratic in range - about 1 percent at
                                  1 m, some 4 cm RMS at 2 m - and
                                  gz-sensors applies only ADDITIVE
                                  gaussian noise to depth; gz-sensors
                                  issue #416 (open since 2024-03-09)
                                  calls that limitation "completely
                                  useless" and proposes multiplicative
                                  and stereo-disparity terms. 8 mm is the
                                  near-field figure, so this camera is
                                  OPTIMISTIC at range by construction.
                                  The quadratic sigma(z) belongs to a
                                  ROS-side refinement in a later phase,
                                  not to this file, and no depth realism
                                  is claimed from the SDF alone.

      THE POINT CLOUD IS NOT BRIDGED. gz's rgbd_camera advertises
      <topic>/points beside the image, the depth image and the camera
      info, and it is welcome to: what F1 carries into ROS is the depth
      image and its camera_info, and a consumer that wants a cloud builds
      one there (docs/reports/m5v3-03 section 5, config.yaml topics).
    -->
    <link name="pallet_cam_link">
      <pose>-0.90 0.40 1.10 0 0.5235988 3.1415927</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.2</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>1e-4</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>1e-4</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>1e-4</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <!-- The housing, BEHIND the lens in this link's own frame, so the
           camera does not render it. Inside the 0.6 m near clip twice
           over. -->
      <visual name="housing">
        <pose>-0.02 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.030 0.090 0.030</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.12 0.13 0.16 1</ambient><diffuse>0.12 0.13 0.16 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <sensor name="pallet_cam" type="rgbd_camera">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>pallet_cam_optical</gz_frame_id>
        <!-- IMAGE FRAME, NOT THE LINK. Gazebo looks along link +X;
             apriltag_ros PnP is ROS optical (Z forward). The dock arm
             publishes pallet_cam_link to pallet_cam_optical. -->
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <topic>/forklift/gz/cam</topic>
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>1.518</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>640</width>
            <height>480</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.6</near>
            <far>6.0</far>
          </clip>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.008</stddev>
          </noise>
        </camera>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- =========================== IMU =========================== -->
    <!--
      MEMS inertial measurement unit. Six axes, no magnetometer, and NO
      ORIENTATION OUTPUT - see the block on <enable_orientation> below,
      which is the most important sentence in this section.

      WHERE IT SITS, AND WHY THERE. On the rear axle centreline
      (x = -0.50, the midpoint of rear_wheel_left and rear_wheel_right)
      and low in the chassis (z = 0.25, just above the 0.20 underside).
      A tricycle's instantaneous centre of rotation always lies on the
      rear axle LINE, whatever the steer angle, so a device mounted on
      that line sees no centripetal term in a steady turn and its
      accelerometer channels stay usable for something later. The FUSED
      quantity does not care: angular rate is the same at every point of
      a rigid body, so yaw rate would read identically anywhere on this
      vehicle. The mounting is chosen for the channels that are not
      fused yet, not for the one that is.

      THE NOISE NUMBERS ARE A DATASHEET, NOT A TUNING. Every value below
      is derived from the Bosch Sensortec BMI088 data sheet,
      BST-BMI088-DS000-19 revision 1.9, 01/2024, tables 4 and 5, read on
      2026-07-31. That device class - a 6-axis consumer/robotics MEMS
      IMU - is what an AGV of this size actually carries. The derivation
      of each number, including the two places where a datasheet figure
      is deliberately NOT used raw, is written out in
      EVIDENCE_ODOMETRY.md section 2. Summary:

        gyro white noise   stddev 0.001745 rad/s
          = the datasheet's own "Output Noise" row, 0.1 deg/s rms at
            BW = 47 Hz. No arithmetic: the sensor is operated at the
            datasheet's own quoted condition (100 Hz ODR, 47 Hz BW).

        gyro bias          bias_mean 0.002618 rad/s, bias_stddev 0
          = TCO 0.015 deg/s/K x 10 K = 0.15 deg/s. NOT the datasheet's
            +-1 deg/s zero-rate offset: that row is measured with
            "slow and fast offset cancellation off", and every AGV
            integration estimates the stationary bias at power-up. What
            survives that estimate is the temperature change since it,
            and 10 K is a device calibrated at power-up in an unheated
            hall and run through a shift. bias_stddev 0 fixes the
            MAGNITUDE across runs; gz still draws the SIGN at random.

        accel white noise  stddev 0.01076 m/s^2 (x, y), 0.01278 (z)
          = noise density 160 ug/sqrt(Hz) (x, y) and 190 (z) at
            sqrt(47 Hz), times g = 9.80665.

        accel bias         bias_mean 0.01961 m/s^2 (= 2 mg)
          = TCO 0.2 mg/K x 10 K, by the same argument as the gyro: the
            20 mg zero-g offset is removed by a levelling calibration,
            the temperature term is not.

      WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, STATED RATHER THAN OMITTED. The datasheet
      publishes no Allan-variance bias instability, so NO in-run bias
      random walk is modelled (<dynamic_bias_stddev> is absent, not
      zero-by-accident). The modelled bias is constant within a run.
      Real drift is therefore worse than what this model produces, and
      every drift figure measured against it is a LOWER BOUND. Inventing
      a random-walk number to close that gap would be a fabricated
      datum, which is worse than a stated limitation.

      <enable_orientation>false</enable_orientation> IS LOAD BEARING.
      gz's IMU derives its orientation output from the LINK'S POSE IN
      THE SIMULATOR - it is ground truth wearing a sensor's name, and
      a real strapdown IMU with no magnetometer cannot produce an
      absolute heading at all. Fusing it would put ground truth back
      into the estimator through the side door and recreate exactly the
      circularity the two-phase odometry plan exists to remove (brief
      m5-07c, forbidden list).

      THE ELEMENT IS HONOURED. WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY ABOUT THE
      MESSAGE IS NOT TRUE, AND IT WAS MEASURED (EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 5).
      It claimed the message would carry orientation_covariance[0] = -1,
      the ROS convention for "no orientation in this message", so that a
      consumer which tried could not silently succeed. On gz-sim 8.11.0
      with ros_gz on Jazzy it does not. What actually arrives is:

        gz side   the gz.msgs.IMU message has NO orientation field at all
                  - protobuf omits it - and orientation_covariance is
                  nine zeros.
        ROS side  sensor_msgs/Imu carries the default-constructed
                  quaternion (0, 0, 0, 0), which is not a unit quaternion
                  and not a rotation, and orientation_covariance is nine
                  zeros. NOT -1.

      So the refusal this element makes is real - there is no oracle
      heading on this topic - but it is NOT self-announcing, and a
      consumer that reads the field without checking gets a zero
      quaternion rather than a documented refusal. That is why the SECOND
      refusal is not redundant: ekf.yaml sets all three orientation
      entries of imu0_config false, and on this stack it is the only one
      of the two a careless consumer cannot walk past.
    -->
    <link name="imu_link">
      <pose>-0.50 0 0.25 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.05</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>1e-5</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>1e-5</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>1e-5</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="imu" type="imu">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <gz_frame_id>imu_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <topic>/forklift/gz/imu</topic>
        <imu>
          <enable_orientation>false</enable_orientation>
          <angular_velocity>
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.001745</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.002618</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.001745</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.002618</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.001745</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.002618</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.01076</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.01961</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.01076</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.01961</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.01278</stddev>
                <bias_mean>0.01961</bias_mean>
                <bias_stddev>0.0</bias_stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <!-- ========================== Mast ========================== -->
    <!-- Fixed structure. The carriage slides on it, see mast_joint. -->
    <link name="mast">
      <inertial>
        <pose>-0.78 0 1.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>120.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>45.63</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>40.40</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>6.03</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="mast_collision">
        <pose>-0.78 0 1.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.10 0.72 2.00</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="mast_rail_left">
        <pose>-0.78 0.30 1.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.09 0.09 2.00</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</ambient><diffuse>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="mast_rail_right">
        <pose>-0.78 -0.30 1.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.09 0.09 2.00</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</ambient><diffuse>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="mast_crossmember">
        <pose>-0.78 0 2.00 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.09 0.69 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</ambient><diffuse>0.14 0.15 0.18 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <!-- THE PALLET CAMERA'S BRACKET, IN THE LINK IT IS BOLTED TO. The
           camera itself is pallet_cam_link, whose pose carries a yaw and
           a pitch; geometry authored in that frame could not be read
           against the carriage sweep, and clearing the carriage is the
           whole reason this mounting has the shape it does. So the two
           boxes live here, where they are axis-aligned in the model
           frame and every clearance is arithmetic:

             lateral plate  x -0.749 to -0.735, y 0.300 to 0.430
                            takes hold of mast_rail_left (x -0.825 to
                            -0.735, y 0.255 to 0.345) and stops 1 mm
                            short of the carriage's -0.750 face
             arm            x -0.900 to -0.735, y 0.4075 to 0.4325
                            runs out past the carriage at a y the
                            carriage's 0.375 half-width never reaches

           Visual only, like the guard and the scanner housings: four
           thin brackets would cost solver time for no observable
           behaviour. They are not invisible to the OTHER sensors, and
           the 3D lidar above sees this one at about 1.2 m. -->
      <visual name="pallet_cam_bracket_plate">
        <pose>-0.742 0.365 1.10 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.014 0.130 0.025</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="pallet_cam_bracket_arm">
        <pose>-0.8175 0.420 1.10 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.165 0.025 0.025</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</ambient><diffuse>0.20 0.21 0.24 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- ==================== Steer and drive ===================== -->
    <!--
      Tricycle front assembly. The steer axis passes through the wheel
      centre, so there is no castor trail to model and the steer angle is
      the wheel heading exactly.
    -->
    <link name="steer_link">
      <pose>0.55 0 0.24 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>18.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0768</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0768</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.0768</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="steer_yoke_visual">
        <geometry><box><size>0.16 0.18 0.22</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</ambient><diffuse>0.22 0.23 0.26 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="drive_wheel">
      <pose>0.55 0 0.12 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>12.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0532</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0864</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.0532</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
          <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- ======================= Rear wheels ====================== -->
    <!-- Passive. No controller is attached to either joint. -->
    <link name="rear_wheel_left">
      <pose>-0.50 0.36 0.12 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>10.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0443</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0720</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.0443</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.4</mu2></ode></friction>
          <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="rear_wheel_right">
      <pose>-0.50 -0.36 0.12 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>10.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0443</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0720</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>0.0443</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.4</mu2></ode></friction>
          <contact><ode><kp>1e6</kp><kd>100.0</kd></ode></contact>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <pose>0 0 0 1.5707963 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.12</radius><length>0.10</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</ambient><diffuse>0.09 0.09 0.10 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- ==================== Carriage and forks =================== -->
    <!-- Link poses below are the carriage at travel zero, fully lowered. -->
    <link name="carriage">
      <pose>-0.80 0 0.35 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>40.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>2.708</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.867</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>1.908</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry><box><size>0.10 0.75 0.50</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry><box><size>0.10 0.75 0.50</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.30 0.32 0.36 1</ambient><diffuse>0.30 0.32 0.36 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="fork_left">
      <pose>-1.35 0.28 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>25.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0352</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>2.302</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>2.327</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry><box><size>1.05 0.12 0.05</size></box></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry><box><size>1.05 0.12 0.05</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.58 0.60 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.58 0.60 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="fork_right">
      <pose>-1.35 -0.28 0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>25.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0352</ixx><ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>2.302</iyy><iyz>0</iyz><izz>2.327</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry><box><size>1.05 0.12 0.05</size></box></geometry>
        <surface>
          <friction><ode><mu>0.9</mu><mu2>0.9</mu2></ode></friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry><box><size>1.05 0.12 0.05</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.58 0.60 0.64 1</ambient><diffuse>0.58 0.60 0.64 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <!-- ========================== Joints ========================= -->

    <joint name="mast_anchor" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>mast</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- Sensor mounts. Fixed, so each scanner's frame is a rigid offset
         of base_link and the transform a consumer needs is a constant. -->
    <joint name="safety_scanner_back_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>safety_scanner_back_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="safety_scanner_left_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>safety_scanner_left_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="safety_scanner_right_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>safety_scanner_right_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="nav_lidar_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>nav_lidar_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="nav_lidar_3d_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>nav_lidar_3d_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- THE ONE SENSOR MOUNT WHOSE PARENT IS NOT base_link. The pallet
         camera is bolted to the mast and its bracket is drawn in the
         mast link, so the joint says the same thing the geometry does.
         mast is itself fixed to base_link by mast_anchor, so the
         transform a consumer needs is still a constant - but if the mast
         ever becomes a moving stage, this camera moves with it and
         nothing here has to be rewritten. -->
    <joint name="pallet_cam_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>mast</parent>
      <child>pallet_cam_link</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="imu_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>imu_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- Steer axis, vertical. Limit is the mechanical stop of the
         assembly, not a control decision. -->
    <joint name="steer_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>steer_link</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1.31</lower>
          <upper>1.31</upper>
          <effort>800</effort>
          <velocity>2.0</velocity>
        </limit>
        <dynamics><damping>80.0</damping><friction>0.5</friction></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- Traction axis. Spin is unlimited; the bounds below are the
         continuous rotation idiom, not a travel limit. -->
    <joint name="drive_wheel_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>steer_link</parent>
      <child>drive_wheel</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e16</lower>
          <upper>1e16</upper>
          <effort>500</effort>
          <velocity>40.0</velocity>
        </limit>
        <dynamics><damping>0.05</damping><friction>0.0</friction></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="rear_wheel_left_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>rear_wheel_left</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e16</lower>
          <upper>1e16</upper>
          <effort>200</effort>
          <velocity>40.0</velocity>
        </limit>
        <dynamics><damping>0.05</damping><friction>0.0</friction></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="rear_wheel_right_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>rear_wheel_right</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 1 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e16</lower>
          <upper>1e16</upper>
          <effort>200</effort>
          <velocity>40.0</velocity>
        </limit>
        <dynamics><damping>0.05</damping><friction>0.0</friction></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!--
      Mast lift. ONE prismatic joint carries the carriage and both tines.
      Travel 0 to 1.6 m is the mechanical stop.

      Two separate things shape the motion, and it is worth being clear
      about which does what, because they are easy to confuse:

        <velocity>0.15</velocity> is the RATE LIMIT and it is enforced by
        the physics engine on the way UP, measured exactly. A raw step on
        /forklift/gz/fork_cmd therefore ramps at 0.15 m/s instead of
        slamming to the target.

        <damping> is the hydraulic flow restriction, and it is what governs
        the way DOWN. The rate limit was measured NOT to bind against a
        gravity assisted descent: with the controller allowed to push down
        hard the carriage came down at 0.170 m/s, through a limit that
        says 0.15. So the descent is not allowed to be a push. cmd_min is
        set so low that lowering is gravity through the restriction, which
        is both how a lift cylinder behaves and the only version of it
        that stays under the declared rate.
    -->
    <joint name="mast_joint" type="prismatic">
      <parent>mast</parent>
      <child>carriage</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>0.0</lower>
          <upper>1.6</upper>
          <effort>12000</effort>
          <velocity>0.15</velocity>
        </limit>
        <dynamics><damping>8000.0</damping><friction>50.0</friction></dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <joint name="fork_left_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>carriage</parent>
      <child>fork_left</child>
    </joint>

    <joint name="fork_right_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>carriage</parent>
      <child>fork_right</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- ====================== gz systems ========================= -->
    <!--
      Every topic below is stated explicitly. None of these systems is
      allowed to fall back on its model scoped default topic, because the
      default embeds the spawned model name and this project treats the
      names as an interface (README.md contract table).
    -->

    <!--
      Steer angle command [rad].

      THE LOAD THIS AXIS WORKS INTO. A steered wheel carrying this vehicle
      scrubs against the floor, a resisting moment measured at roughly
      400 N m. That moment is what sets every number in this plugin, and
      the quantity that decides whether a command executes at all is the
      angle error at which the proportional term first exceeds it:

          e* = M_scrub / p_gain = 400 / 60000 = 0.0067 rad = 0.38 deg

      Below e* the joint moves only as the integral winds up, which takes
      seconds. p_gain is therefore not a tuning preference: it is the
      smallest steer correction the plant can execute promptly.

      WHY p_gain IS 60000 AND NOT 6000. This axis ran at 6000 through M4
      and most of M5, which puts e* at 3.8 deg. Nav2's corrections on a
      straight leg are 1 to 2.5 deg over a leg lasting 8 to 9 s, so none
      of them executed and a straight leg was open loop in yaw: the
      heading error the vehicle entered with was held, and a held heading
      error integrates into cross track at sin(theta) per metre, measured
      at 0.084 m/m mean magnitude over ten routes. Raising p_gain to
      60000, the value the mast joint already uses, moves e* to 0.38 deg,
      below every command the controller forms on a straight leg. Measured
      on the plant bench, a 2 deg command goes from 5 percent executed to
      76 percent executed inside 1.4 s and the body follows the joint to
      within 1 percent; over five route repeats the mean cross track rate
      falls from 0.089 to 0.0134 m/m. See EVIDENCE_NAV2.md sections 11.2,
      11.3 and 11.6 (m5-38) for the static curve, the A/B that located the
      resisting moment in the tyre contact, and the distribution.

      Two honest limits on that number. No upper bracket was swept: 60000
      is a sufficient value derived from this model's own 400 N m figure,
      and its stability is evidenced by the absence of hunting out to
      +/- 20 deg on the bench, not by a sweep. And the residual shortfall
      below 0.5 deg (0.09 to 0.23 of commanded inside a 1.4 s window) is
      settling, not a dead region; it falls smoothly with command size.

      A CLAIM THIS COMMENT USED TO MAKE, AND WHY IT IS GONE. It said the
      scrub "disappears once the vehicle rolls", which is false and was
      falsified directly: rolling at 0.6 m/s in the warehouse, the
      committed axis still failed every command under 3 deg. The variable
      that removes the resisting moment is GRIP, not motion. The same
      bench in the arena world, whose floor gives the drive wheel no
      traction at all, reaches every commanded angle including 0.5 deg
      inside 1.4 s. Anything measured on a surface without grip
      characterises this axis with its load disconnected.

      i_max STAYS AT 800, just above the measured scrub moment, and its
      job has changed. Under p_gain 6000 the integral was the only term
      that could ever break the scrub, so the clamp had to clear 400 N m
      or a permanent error survived; the clamp is still sized that way and
      that lower bound still holds for the standing still case, where a
      loaded wheel is turned from rest. What the clamp no longer carries
      is the running correction, which p_gain now executes directly. The
      upper bound on the clamp is unchanged and was observed: a surplus
      large enough to outlive the condition that built it hunts the joint
      at its rate limit.

      WHERE THE DAMPING LIVES, AND WHY IT IS NOT d_gain. The controller's
      derivative term is an EXPLICIT force evaluated once per physics step,
      so it is stable only while d_gain stays well under inertia / step,
      which for this assembly is about 0.13 / 0.002 = 65. Larger values
      were tried and do not damp: they chatter, and at d_gain 1200 the
      joint stopped responding to its topic altogether. The damping that
      actually holds this joint steady is <dynamics><damping> on the joint
      itself, which the physics engine integrates implicitly. d_gain here
      is a trim term, nothing more.

      Joint damping has its own ceiling, found the same way. At 80 the
      joint slews to a commanded angle and settles; raised to 400 against
      this joint's 800 N m effort limit it stopped moving altogether while
      still reporting a live command. Neither number was predicted; both
      were measured, and the working value is the one below.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
      <joint_name>steer_joint</joint_name>
      <topic>/forklift/gz/actuator/steer_cmd</topic>
      <p_gain>60000.0</p_gain>
      <i_gain>1500.0</i_gain>
      <d_gain>30.0</d_gain>
      <i_max>800.0</i_max>
      <i_min>-800.0</i_min>
      <cmd_max>4000.0</cmd_max>
      <cmd_min>-4000.0</cmd_min>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Traction command [rad/s] at the wheel. Raw velocity, no ramp. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-controller-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointController">
      <joint_name>drive_wheel_joint</joint_name>
      <topic>/forklift/gz/actuator/traction_cmd</topic>
      <initial_velocity>0.0</initial_velocity>
    </plugin>

    <!--
      THE TYRE IS ALLOWED TO SLIP, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE
      PHASE. Measured on this rig before this system was added
      (EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 7, the baseline row): commanded 0.700000 m/s
      of tread, ground truth 0.699957 m/s, slip 0.003 percent. The
      contact was rigid, so a wheel revolution WAS 2 pi r of ground,
      exactly, for ever. Wheel odometry computed off that joint would not
      be an estimate at all - it would be ground truth with extra steps,
      the F2 EKF would have nothing to correct, and every localisation
      figure taken against it would be circular. The same argument the
      OdometryPublisher block at the foot of this file makes about the
      ground-truth transform applies one level down, at the contact.

      WHAT THIS SYSTEM DOES. It gives the drive wheel's contact patch a
      finite compliance in the two ground-plane directions, so the patch
      creeps under load the way a polyurethane tyre on concrete does
      instead of gripping perfectly. Only the DRIVEN wheel carries it:
      the two rear wheels are passive and their slip would be a claim
      nothing here measures.

      CORRECTED BY MEASUREMENT 2026-08-26 (F1.5), and the paragraph above
      is kept because it is what this file believed and it is exactly the
      belief that was wrong. EVIDENCE_LATERAL_TUNE.md 2 measures the
      corner the plugin never had: at 0.3 m/s and pi/4 of steer the truck
      delivered 0.410 of the yaw its geometry promises, and the split of
      that deficit is 99.5 percent at the STEERED wheel's contact and
      0.5 percent at the rear axle. The rear axle was not sliding - and
      that was the whole problem. Two rigid contact patches 0.10 m wide
      cannot be yawed about their own vertical axes without sliding, so
      with no compliance on them they resisted the vehicle's yaw with a
      Coulomb couple the steered wheel then had to overcome by sliding
      sideways. Measured, one variable at a time, on this rig:

        rear wheels with NO entry here (the paragraph above)  0.410
        the same 7.0 given to both of them                    1.005

      So "their slip would be a claim nothing here measures" was true of
      the number and false of the OMISSION: leaving the entry out is not
      neutral, it asserts a rigid tyre, and a rigid tyre on a vehicle
      that turns is the stronger claim. All three contacts carry the
      plugin now, at the SAME compliance value, and NOT because the three
      wheels are the same tyre - they are not, quite: the rear pair's
      friction reads mu 0.9 / mu2 0.4 against the drive wheel's 1.0 /
      0.9. What is shared is the thing the compliance value is a property
      of. This system's slip is param x radius x |spin| / normal force,
      and mu appears nowhere in it: it is a give per newton of load, so
      the same value on three wheels of the same 0.12 m radius is the
      same GIVE, scaled by whatever each wheel is carrying. That is what
      wheel_normal_force below is for, and it is why 7.0 needed no
      re-tuning to be handed to the rear pair. Their mu is a separate
      parameter, unchanged by this gate and out of its scope.

      THE TWO PHYSICAL PARAMETERS ARE THIS MODEL'S OWN NUMBERS, READ OFF
      IT, NOT CHOSEN.

        wheel_radius 0.12   the drive_wheel collision cylinder's radius,
                            above in this file. It is not a parameter of
                            this system, it is a fact about the wheel.

        wheel_normal_force  the STATIC load this wheel carries. The
                            sixteen links of this model total 1165.95 kg;
                            summing mass x inertial-x over all of them
                            gives -97.151 kg m, so the centre of mass is
                            at x = -0.083323 m. The drive contact is at
                            x = +0.55 and the rear axle at x = -0.50, a
                            1.05 m wheelbase, so as a static beam

                              W       = 1165.95 x 9.80665 = 11434.06 N
                              N_drive = W x (0.50 - 0.083323) / 1.05
                                      = 11434.06 x 0.396835
                                      = 4537.4 N

                            Treating the 50 kg of wheel and steer yoke as
                            unsprung and carrying it straight to the
                            ground instead gives 4537.4 N as well: the
                            two agree to 0.01 N, so the beam is used and
                            the distinction is not drawn. Roughly 39
                            percent of the vehicle on one wheel, which is
                            what a counterbalance truck with its load
                            end empty does.

                            THE REAR PAIR, ADDED 2026-08-26 (F1.5), by
                            the same beam and out of the same numbers:
                              N_rear_total = W - N_drive
                                           = 11434.06 - 4537.4
                                           = 6896.66 N
                            and the model's centre of mass is on the
                            centreline, so it halves:
                              N_rear_each  = 3448.3 N
                            Two wheels at 0.36 m either side of y = 0,
                            30 percent of the vehicle each, which with
                            the drive wheel's 39 percent accounts for
                            the whole 1165.95 kg and nothing else.
                              AND THE SUM IS THE COMPOSED ONE, CHECKED
                              2026-08-26: SDFormat reads an
                              <inertial><pose> in the LINK frame, so a
                              link's centre of mass is its <link><pose>
                              composed with it. NO LINK IN THIS MODEL
                              HAS BOTH: fourteen carry a <link><pose>
                              and no <inertial><pose>, and base_link and
                              mast carry an <inertial><pose> and no
                              <link><pose>. So nothing composes with
                              anything anywhere here, and those two
                              inertial poses ARE their model-frame
                              positions. Summed the
                              other way - link poses alone, which would
                              drop base_link's +0.10 and the mast's
                              -0.78 - the total reads -93.551 kg m, and
                              it is not what the -97.151 above is. So
                              the -97.151, the -0.083323 and the 4537.4
                              are already composed and already right.
                              EVIDENCE_LATERAL_TUNE.md 7 carries the
                              check, and the retraction of a claim to
                              the contrary that this comment briefly
                              made on the same day.

      THE TWO COMPLIANCES ARE MEASURED, AND THEY HAD TO BE. gz's slip
      compliance is unitless and what it produces depends on the contact
      solver, so no arithmetic gets from a target slip to a number: the
      only honest route is to command the cruise and read the ground
      truth. tools/slip_bench.sh is that instrument and
      EVIDENCE_MODEL_V3.md 7 is the tuning table, rejected values
      included. The target is 0.5 to 2 percent at the 0.7 m/s cruise
      m6/ipc/follower.py drives.

      LATERAL IS HELD EQUAL TO LONGITUDINAL RATHER THAN TUNED. This track
      measures no lateral manoeuvre yet - the bench drives straight - and
      a number tuned against nothing measured is a number invented. It is
      written down as a value this file does not stand behind, and the
      phase that measures a turn is the phase that gets to change it.

      AND THE TWO ELEMENT NAMES ARE THE WRONG WAY ROUND, MEASURED. On
      this rig it is <slip_compliance_lateral> that governs this wheel's
      ROLLING direction: moved alone it takes the straight-line
      longitudinal slip from 0.96162 to 0.10688 and 0.01070 percent,
      while <slip_compliance_longitudinal> moved alone leaves it at
      0.95842 and 0.96140 - unchanged. Scope of that reading, so it is
      not over-read: gz-sim 8.11.0, the gz-sim-wheel-slip-system as
      configured below, this vehicle, this rig; whether the plugin emits
      the pair in the other order or the solver's friction directions are
      simply not the wheel's was not chased. EVIDENCE_LATERAL_TUNE.md 3.1
      is the measurement. It reaches nothing while both are 7.0 and the
      contact is isotropic - and it is a trap the moment anyone sets them
      apart, which is why it is written here beside the values and not
      only in an evidence file.

      THAT PHASE WAS F1.5, AND IT LEFT THE NUMBER WHERE IT WAS. The
      turn is measured now (EVIDENCE_LATERAL_TUNE.md) and lateral 7.0 is
      no longer a value this file does not stand behind - it is the
      value the corner selects. The ladder is in that file with every
      rejected row; the three that matter here are that dropping the
      steered wheel's lateral compliance to 1.0 took the delivered
      fraction from 0.410 only to 0.713 and to 0.1 took it BACK DOWN to
      0.697, while giving the rear wheels the compliance they never had
      took it to 1.005 with the lateral left at 7.0.
        AND EQUAL IS NOT A COINCIDENCE, IT IS THE READING. Every value
        that made one direction of a contact stiffer than the other
        brought back the heading dependence EVIDENCE_SENSORS.md 4.2
        measures: the yaw rate inside one held corner wanders 10.1
        percent of its own mean on the untuned plant, 1.0 percent with
        the steered wheel at lateral 3.0 / longitudinal 7.0, and 0.0
        percent (sd 0.000045 rad/s over nine 1 s bins) with all six
        compliances equal. That is what EVIDENCE_SENSORS.md 4.2's named
        and unchased candidate predicts - an axis-aligned friction
        pyramid, which is a set of directions the WHEEL does not turn
        with - and an anisotropic contact patch on such a solver is a
        tyre whose grip depends on which way the truck is pointing. The
        mechanism is still not chased here; what is measured is that six
        equal numbers is the only setting free of it.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-wheel-slip-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::WheelSlip">
      <wheel link_name="drive_wheel">
        <slip_compliance_lateral>7.0</slip_compliance_lateral>
        <slip_compliance_longitudinal>7.0</slip_compliance_longitudinal>
        <wheel_normal_force>4537.4</wheel_normal_force>
        <wheel_radius>0.12</wheel_radius>
      </wheel>
      <wheel link_name="rear_wheel_left">
        <slip_compliance_lateral>7.0</slip_compliance_lateral>
        <slip_compliance_longitudinal>7.0</slip_compliance_longitudinal>
        <wheel_normal_force>3448.3</wheel_normal_force>
        <wheel_radius>0.12</wheel_radius>
      </wheel>
      <wheel link_name="rear_wheel_right">
        <slip_compliance_lateral>7.0</slip_compliance_lateral>
        <slip_compliance_longitudinal>7.0</slip_compliance_longitudinal>
        <wheel_normal_force>3448.3</wheel_normal_force>
        <wheel_radius>0.12</wheel_radius>
      </wheel>
    </plugin>

    <!--
      Carriage travel command [m], measured from fully lowered.

      The clamps are ASYMMETRIC, and deliberately so: gravity is. A lift
      needs a large integral in the LIFTING direction, because holding the
      carriage still is the integral's job once the position error has
      gone to nothing, and it needs almost none in the lowering direction,
      because lowering is what gravity does for free.

      WHICH CLAMP IS WHICH IS NOT WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE. gz's PID forms its
      error as (position minus target) and negates the sum, so while the
      carriage sits BELOW its target the integral term is NEGATIVE and it
      is i_min, the seemingly downward clamp, that bounds how hard the
      lift can push UP. Sized the intuitive way round, with i_min small,
      the carriage was measured to stop and hold 15.7 mm short of an
      0.80 m target, because the integral was allowed only 200 N of the
      882 N the assembly weighs and the rest had to come out of position
      error. The pairing below is the corrected one.

      p_gain is large for the reason a lift needs it: the last centimetre
      of an approach is where the proportional term has to overcome the
      whole weight to stop the carriage, and a soft gain buys that
      stopping distance back in overshoot.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-position-controller-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointPositionController">
      <joint_name>mast_joint</joint_name>
      <topic>/forklift/gz/actuator/fork_cmd</topic>
      <p_gain>60000.0</p_gain>
      <i_gain>2500.0</i_gain>
      <d_gain>9000.0</d_gain>
      <i_max>200.0</i_max>
      <i_min>-1500.0</i_min>
      <cmd_max>3000.0</cmd_max>
      <cmd_min>-100.0</cmd_min>
    </plugin>

    <!--
      THE IMU SYSTEM, CARRIED BY THE MODEL AND NOT BY THE WORLD.

      gz's IMU sensor produces nothing unless some system creates it, the
      same way a gpu_lidar produces nothing unless gz-sim-sensors-system
      is loaded. The usual placement for BOTH is the world file - and for
      the rendering sensors it has to be the world, because they need a
      render engine the world owns.

      The IMU system does NOT: it scans the whole entity-component
      manager for Imu components regardless of which entity it was
      attached to, so a model-level declaration serves this model. That
      was measured, not assumed - EVIDENCE_ODOMETRY.md section 3 records
      the same model spawned into a world with no IMU system of its own,
      publishing /forklift/gz/imu.

      This is worth the two lines because of what it avoids: no world
      file has to be edited to give this vehicle an IMU, so the sensor
      travels with the model into sim/worlds/forklift_arena.sdf,
      sim/worlds/warehouse.sdf and any world a later gate adds. The
      vehicle carries its own instruments. Contrast the four scanners,
      which go silent in a world that forgot gz-sim-sensors-system and
      have needed that noted in every launch file since.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Imu">
    </plugin>

    <!--
      Joint feedback. Position and rate of the three driven joints.

      This system has NO rate parameter and publishes once per physics
      iteration; an <update_rate> child was tried and measured to change
      nothing. The rate is therefore the world's, about 493 Hz in a 500 Hz
      world, and a world that bridges this topic into ROS is choosing that
      traffic. The vehicle nodes do not need it: they consume it at their
      own timer rate and drop the rest.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <topic>/forklift/gz/joint_state</topic>
      <joint_name>steer_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>drive_wheel_joint</joint_name>
      <joint_name>mast_joint</joint_name>
    </plugin>

    <!--
      THE SAFE-SPEED ENCODER: ONE SHAFT, TWO READING CHANNELS.

      The two systems below read the SAME joint, drive_wheel_joint, and
      publish it on two separate topics. That is the whole of what this
      file contributes to the vehicle's speed measurement, and the shape
      is deliberate: it models a safe encoder, which is ONE device on ONE
      shaft carrying TWO reading channels whose values an external safe
      control cross-compares.

      ITS HONEST NAME IS A SINGLE-CHANNEL TESTED SYSTEM, and that name is
      used in every artefact that describes it (config.yaml safe_speed,
      scripts/safe_speed_channels.py, EVIDENCE_ODOMETRY.md section 14).
      One shaft, one measured quantity, two readings of it. Calling the
      arrangement "two-channel" would claim a redundancy it does not
      have: both readings die together with the shaft they read, which
      is exactly why the design carries a separate motion-present
      observation beside them (see below).

      WHY TWO PLUGIN INSTANCES AND NOT ONE TOPIC READ TWICE. A subscriber
      that reads one topic twice has one reading path, and a fault in it
      is common to both copies - the cross-comparison would then compare
      a number with itself and pass for ever. Two instances give two
      publishers, two message streams and two subscriptions, so the
      failure of one read path is visible to the comparison. What the
      instances do NOT give is independent noise: they publish the same
      simulator value, because it IS the same shaft. The independence
      that makes a cross-comparison mean anything lives in the reading
      heads, and the reading heads are modelled in
      scripts/safe_speed_channels.py - each with its own mounting phase
      on the count grid and its own read jitter. This file supplies the
      shaft; the node supplies the heads.

      WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a safe measurement, it has no
      integrity, no diagnostic coverage and no fault reaction, and no PL,
      Category, SIL or PFH is claimed for it or for anything downstream
      of it. The readings reach the F-program as STANDARD DATA over the
      process path, which is a stand-in for a safe measurement channel
      and is labelled one wherever it appears. The same rule as the
      scanners: what this file supplies is the GEOMETRY and the SIGNAL of
      a real installation, never a safety property.

      THE MOTION-PRESENT CHECK IS NOT IN THIS FILE, AND IT IS A STAND-IN.
      Two readings of one shaft lie together if the shaft or its coupling
      fails, so a claimed zero speed is corroborated against an
      observation that does not pass through the shaft at all. Real
      systems close that hole with a MECHANICAL FAULT EXCLUSION argued on
      the coupling - a construction argument, not a monitored signal.
      This project has no such argument available, so it substitutes an
      observation and says so. The observation is derived from the
      navigation lidar by scripts/safe_speed_channels.py, and it is
      labelled a stand-in in every artefact that carries it.

      ADDITIVE, AND MEASURED TO BE SO (m5-48). These two systems are
      read-only: JointStatePublisher publishes joint state and writes
      nothing the physics reads. No link, joint, inertial, geometry,
      sensor, gain, limit or existing topic changed with them, and the
      joint-position and joint-velocity components they require are
      already created for this same joint by the system above, so not
      even a component is added. The one class of committed figure an
      additive edit can still move is anything derived from machine
      load, so that was measured rather than argued: three alternating
      60 s captures of the simulator's own real-time factor, with and
      without these two systems, gave mean RTF 0.9903 both ways
      (EVIDENCE_ODOMETRY.md section 14.2).
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <topic>/forklift/gz/drive_speed/read_a</topic>
      <joint_name>drive_wheel_joint</joint_name>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher">
      <topic>/forklift/gz/drive_speed/read_b</topic>
      <joint_name>drive_wheel_joint</joint_name>
    </plugin>

    <!--
      GROUND-TRUTH odometry, and - FOR NOW - the odom -> base_link
      transform. Read the two-phase note before using either.

      THIS IS GROUND TRUTH, NOT A WHEEL ESTIMATE. This system reads the
      model's pose out of the simulator, so the pose it reports and the
      transform it publishes carry NO odometry error: no wheel slip, no
      encoder quantisation, no unbounded heading drift. A real vehicle's
      odom -> base_link drifts without bound; this one does not, ever.

      TWO PHASES, AND THIS FILE IS PHASE ONE.

        Phase 1, now (M5, brief m5-07b). Nothing on this vehicle can
        estimate its own motion yet, and SLAM cannot start without a
        motion estimate, so the ground-truth transform is bridged onto
        /tf as an INTERIM SOURCE. It is a scaffold, not the answer.

        Phase 2, next (owner ruling, 2026-07-31). An IMU joins this
        model, wheel odometry is computed from the joint states with
        slip and noise modelled, and an EKF fuses the two. THE EKF THEN
        OWNS odom -> base_link. This system keeps publishing, and its
        role becomes permanent and different: it is the REFERENCE that
        localisation error is measured AGAINST, and it stops being an
        input to anything that navigates.

      WHY PHASE 2 IS NOT OPTIONAL. With ground-truth odometry the
      degenerate aisle stretches of the arena cannot bite, AMCL has
      nothing to correct, and any "error against ground truth" figure is
      circular - AMCL scored against its own input. Ground truth is only
      a reference while something ELSE is the estimate.

      THE SEAM, so the change is a switch and not a rewrite. The gz
      transform topic is named <tf_topic> below and carried onto /tf by
      one dedicated bridge node in launch/vehicle.launch.py, behind the
      launch argument ground_truth_tf. When the EKF lands it is started
      with ground_truth_tf:=false: this system keeps publishing its
      topics, nothing here is deleted, and the ONLY publisher of
      odom -> base_link becomes the EKF. Nothing else in this file
      changes.

      ONE OWNER PER DATUM AT ANY TIME (invariant 10). In phase 1 the
      transform is published by THIS system, from the same pose that
      fills the odometry message, rather than by a node that recomputes
      a pose from joint states - two sources would be two answers to one
      question with nothing to say which is right. In phase 2 that one
      owner is the EKF, and the switch above is what makes the handover
      exclusive rather than additive.

      WHY THE TOPIC IS NAMED HERE, AND WHY THE NAME SAYS "ground_truth".
      This system publishes the transform whether or not <tf_topic> is
      present; without it the name is the MODEL-SCOPED default
      /model/<spawn name>/pose, which changes when the model is spawned
      under another name - exactly what the COMMAND TOPICS note above
      refuses for every other system in this file. Measured before this
      element was added: the default topic was /model/Forklift/pose,
      gz.msgs.Pose_V, carrying frame_id "forklift/odom" and
      child_frame_id "forklift/base_link". The name carries
      ground_truth because after phase 2 there are TWO pose streams on
      this vehicle, and a reader must never have to guess which one is
      the reference.

      The frames are the model's own <odom_frame> and <robot_base_frame>,
      unscoped and unaltered, so the transform lands on the same tree the
      sensor frames of scripts/sensor_tf.py hang from - and so that the
      EKF can take the same edge over without renaming a frame.
    -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher">
      <odom_topic>/forklift/gz/odom</odom_topic>
      <tf_topic>/forklift/gz/tf_ground_truth</tf_topic>
      <odom_frame>forklift/odom</odom_frame>
      <robot_base_frame>forklift/base_link</robot_base_frame>
      <dimensions>2</dimensions>
      <odom_publish_frequency>20</odom_publish_frequency>
    </plugin>

  </model>
</sdf>
